/* Logic :

If both nodes are NULL

Both trees have no node at this position.
So they are the same → true.
If only one node is NULL

One tree has a node while the other doesn't.
So the trees are different → false.
Compare their values

If p->val and q->val are different → false.
Compare the left subtrees

Recursively check whether p->left and q->left are the same.
Compare the right subtrees

Recursively check whether p->right and q->right are the same.
Both sides must be the same

If left and right subtrees are both identical, return true.
Easy way to remember
NULL → NULL = same
One NULL = different
Different values = different
Otherwise → check left + check right

So the pattern is:

Check current nodes → check left subtree → check right subtree.

*/

class Solution {
public:
    bool isSameTree(TreeNode* p, TreeNode* q) {

        // If both nodes are NULL,
        // there is nothing to compare, so they are the same
        if (p == nullptr && q == nullptr)
            return true;

        // If only one node is NULL,
        // one tree has a node and the other doesn't
        // so the trees are different
        if (p == nullptr || q == nullptr)
            return false;

        // If the values of the current nodes are different,
        // the trees are not the same
        if (p->val != q->val)
            return false;

        // Compare the left subtrees
        // and compare the right subtrees
        //
        // Both must be the same for the complete trees
        // to be identical
        return isSameTree(p->left, q->left) && isSameTree(p->right, q->right);
    }
};
